drm: h713: afbd: the RGB channel over the video plane

The primary plane (AFBD channel 2, OSD-B) can lie over the video plane
instead of being swapped against it, as stock lays Android's interface over
the HDMI picture: the stock HWC commits every frame to ge2d osd_id 1, which is
this channel, and ge2d's tgd_put_plane_info() sets the downstream selector to
(old & 0xd6ffffff) | 0x21000000 for it - bit 28, the video, stays.

The selector is not the exclusive mux the driver assumed since 0078: bit 28
alone shows source 0; without it the RGB channel lies over source 0, at the
global alpha in LVDS + 0x070 with bit 27 (0xff: opaque, the console as it has
always looked) and at each pixel's alpha without it. The byte order is the
plane block word 0x0524c01c [15:8], two bits per output component naming the
source byte; the boot loader's LogoRegData leaves 0x06 there (XRGB colours,
alpha from the blue byte), ARGB8888 needs 0xc6 (ge2d writes 0xe4 for Android's
RGBA8888).

The primary plane gets a zpos property (0..2, default 0) and the video plane
an immutable zpos 1. At zpos 2 the RGB channel stays live while source 0 is
on: ARGB8888 with per-pixel alpha (selector 0x21000000, byte order 0xc6),
XRGB8888 opaque on top. At the default nothing changes - the video plane's
enable still quiesces RGB first, and a client that never sets zpos sees the
driver it had. The plane block window is a new, optional "osd" reg entry; a
tree without it probes as before and refuses an ARGB primary above the video.

Measured 10.10.2026 on the HY310 through /dev/mem with the warp running over
a 1080p60 HDMI source (arbeit/osd-a-20261010, runs K1-K7): channel 2 on with
selector 0x21000000 over the video plane, 30 s at 59.3 frames a second with
no frame dropped and no extra CPU, per-pixel alpha 0x00/0x80/0xff right,
colours right with 0xc6. Not yet measured: this patch on the device.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
